WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:02.340
What do sudoku games,

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:03.841
wedding seating plans,

00:00:03.841 --> 00:00:07.265
and assigning frequencies to radio stations all have in common.

00:00:07.265 --> 00:00:10.680
They are all related
to a well-known math problem,

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:12.030
connected to maps.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.530
A proof of this problem was the very
first that relied heavily on a computer.

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:22.013
This breakthrough had a huge impact on
the fields of mathematics and computer science.

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:26.460
But it also ignited decades of debate
about the nature of mathematical proofs,

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:31.290
the role of computers in the field, and
what it means to be a mathematician.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.300
The long and winding journey
started with a simple question.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:40.620
Imagine you had a blank map
and you're a cartographer,

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:42.240
and you wanted to color the map.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:47.220
So one thing that might be important is
that each region have its own color and

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:50.610
that no two regions that share
a border are the same color.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.490
And so the question is, how
many colors will you need?

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:57.150
It sounds like a simple
question. How hard can it be?

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:59.850
History surrounding it
is, without a doubt.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:02.580
One of my favorite episodes
in the history of math.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:05.010
One might think that this
problem came from cartographers,

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:07.223
but there's really no evidence that mapmakers

00:01:07.223 --> 00:01:09.526
have been trying to minimize the number of colors.

00:01:09.536 --> 00:01:12.420
Um, this really came from mathematicians.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:18.090
In 1852, Augustus de Morgan, a
professor at University College London,

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.600
brought up the map coloring problem
in a letter to his friend and the

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:24.268
mathematician, Sir William Rowan Hamilton.

00:01:24.268 --> 00:01:28.560
It was De Morgan's former student, Francis Guthrie, who
first posed the question.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:33.200
Guthrie had discovered that he could
shade in all the counties in the United Kingdom

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.200
with only four colors.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:39.060
He wondered if this was the case for
all maps. Regardless of the shape, size,

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:40.080
and complexity.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.820
The simplicity of the Four Color Theorem,
that only four colors are needed to

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:45.803
color any map,

00:01:45.803 --> 00:01:48.123
tantalized mathematicians.

00:01:48.123 --> 00:01:51.507
However, early attempts to prove it failed.

00:01:51.507 --> 00:01:57.690
27 years later, the first widely accepted proof came
from the British barrister, Alfred Kempe.

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:02.070
His proof tackled the problem by analyzing
how countries connect to each other.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:06.000
To understand how Kemp and other
mathematicians looked at this problem,

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:09.300
it helps to strip away all
unnecessary information.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:13.890
We can replace each state
by a vertex, a single point.

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:16.830
And if two regions neighbor each other,

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:21.540
we can draw a line segment
between those two vertices.

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:22.560
That's called an edge.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:25.670
And so we turn this from a map coloring problem

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:27.825
into a graph coloring problem.

00:02:27.825 --> 00:02:32.640
Can we color the graph with four or
fewer colors so that neighboring vertices

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:33.750
are not the same color?

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.140
This field of math is
known as graph theory,

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.010
and it helps mathematicians study
the relationships between objects.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.350
Converting maps to planar graphs,

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:48.150
graphs that can be drawn in a flat plane
with no edges crossing makes the four

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:50.160
color theorem easier to analyze.

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:55.350
It turns out that two maps of different
sizes and shapes can be represented by

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:56.790
equivalent planar graphs.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:01.000
All that matters is how the regions
are connected and related to one another.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:05.170
Focusing on only the connections within
the maps helped Kempe discover key

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:07.090
features that all maps share.

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:09.578
Regardless of how complicated,

00:03:09.578 --> 00:03:15.550
all maps have, at least one region
that has 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:16.813
or 5 neighbors.

00:03:16.813 --> 00:03:19.420
This is known as an unavoidable set.

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:23.770
Once Kempe had identified
an unavoidable set,

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:26.230
he set out to prove
the Four Color Theorem.

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:29.530
To understand a surprising
step of his method, first,

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:31.450
assume that the theorem is false.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:34.780
Kemp's proof is elegant and clever.

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:39.580
Suppose we cannot color every
map with four or fewer colors,

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:41.890
then there must be a smallest such map.

00:03:42.310 --> 00:03:44.110
If the Four Color Theorem is false,

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:47.320
there must be a graph that
requires at least five colors.

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:50.080
This is known as a
minimal counter example.

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:54.435
Kemp wanted to show that he could color
this graph with four colors.

00:03:54.435 --> 00:03:55.210
If he could,

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:59.099
then a minimal counter example must
not exist in the first place.

00:03:59.099 --> 00:04:00.010
First,

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:04.409
he found one vertex in that graph that
had five or fewer neighbors and removed it.

00:04:04.409 --> 00:04:10.046
Then he colored the graph with only
four colors because the graph is smaller than the minimal counter example.

00:04:10.046 --> 00:04:13.180
Next, he put the removed vertex back in.

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:16.270
If its neighbors show
three or fewer colors,

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:18.730
there is a color remaining
to color the vertex.

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:22.930
But if the neighbors have all four
colors, then there is a problem.

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:27.670
Kempe then created a method for recoloring
the graph to free up a color for the

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:28.635
remaining vertex.

00:04:28.635 --> 00:04:32.140
This allowed him to
recolor it with four colors again.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:34.390
This showed that a
minimal counter example,

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:38.620
the smallest possible graph that
needs five colors doesn't exist.

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:41.740
That meant the Four Color
Theorem must be true.

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:47.506
Unfortunately, 11 years later, an
error was discovered.

00:04:47.506 --> 00:04:51.604
Mathematician Percy Haewood found that Kempe's
recoloring technique worked

00:04:51.604 --> 00:04:56.500
when the removed vertex had four or fewer neighbors,
but not when it had five.

00:04:57.010 --> 00:04:58.990
When you read Haewood's writing,

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:02.080
you could tell he feels bad for
having discovered this flaw.

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:05.053
So the theorem was a conjecture once
again.

00:05:05.053 --> 00:05:10.060
For the next half century, mathematicians continued trying
to prove the four color theorem.

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:14.620
They soon realized that any solution
would require a two-pronged approach.

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:18.220
First, they needed to expand
Kemp's unavoidable set.

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:23.350
This is a collection of configurations
that if you have any graph,

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:26.680
then one of these configurations
must appear in your graph.

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:30.910
The more they looked, the bigger and
more complicated these sets became.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:35.080
The second task was to prove that
these configurations were reducible,

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:38.710
meaning that if a graph contained
one of these configurations,

00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:41.530
then it could be colored
with four or fewer colors.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:47.050
And proving that these configurations
were reducible also was

00:05:47.050 --> 00:05:47.920
harder and harder.

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:51.940
Fortunately, by the end of
the 1960s and early seventies,

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:54.790
computers were becoming
faster and more powerful,

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:57.430
opening the door to new possibilities.

00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:00.260
Mathematicians realized
that some of this work,

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:03.890
which was very tedious and
time consuming to do by hand,

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:08.390
might be able to be accomplished
by the use of these new computers.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:09.570
At this point,

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:13.400
there were several teams trying to solve
the Four Color Problem using computers

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:18.230
At the University of Illinois. Wolfgang
Haken, a well-known topologist,

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:21.260
teamed up with Kenneth Appel,
a mathematics professor,

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:26.063
and one of the only people who knew how
to use the university's new high-powered computers.

00:06:26.063 --> 00:06:31.970
In 1976, after about six months of work and more
than a thousand hours of computer

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:34.490
time, they proved the Four Color Theorem.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:38.240
Haken and Appel found an
unavoidable set consisting of

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:41.930
1,936 reducible configurations.

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:45.230
Every map has to have at least
one of these configurations,

00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:49.460
and any map that does can be
colored with four or fewer colors.

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:55.603
Far too many for any human or
even team of humans to check by hand.

00:06:56.600 --> 00:07:00.628
At the time, computers were primarily used to perform
mathematical calculations

00:07:00.628 --> 00:07:06.229
and not for what was considered the human endeavor
of solving proofs in theoretical mathematics.

00:07:06.229 --> 00:07:09.560
As the first major computer assisted proof,

00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:13.700
the Four Color Theorem raised questions
about what actually counts as a proof.

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:17.780
Some researchers even questioned the
legitimacy of Appel and Hakens' method.

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:23.240
But if we put ourselves back in the
mindset of 1970s mathematicians,

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:27.920
this idea that one of the most
famous unsolved problems in history

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:31.850
was solved by over a thousand
hours of computer time,

00:07:32.030 --> 00:07:36.200
that seems sort of anticlimactic
or not playing by the rules,

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:38.720
these unstated rules
that mathematicians have.

00:07:39.410 --> 00:07:42.500
Some people thought that if a human
couldn't read it from end to end,

00:07:42.770 --> 00:07:45.144
then it wasn't a legitimate proof.

00:07:45.144 --> 00:07:48.874
But as time progressed, the use of computers and theoretical mathematics

00:07:48.874 --> 00:07:50.690
became more accepted.

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:52.700
As a younger generation got older,

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:56.579
this newfangled technology just became
the way that we did math.

00:07:56.579 --> 00:08:00.080
And uh, today we're much more comfortable
with it than we were at that time.

00:08:00.650 --> 00:08:05.390
Today, mathematicians often use computers
to check proofs of theorems or even to

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:06.740
generate proofs themselves.

00:08:07.250 --> 00:08:11.390
This fruitful and continuing marriage
of mathematics and computers all started

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:14.630
with a seemingly simple
question of how to color maps.

00:08:15.170 --> 00:08:18.260
Through the study of this problem

00:08:18.350 --> 00:08:21.590
mathematics was created and
advanced in so many ways.

00:08:21.800 --> 00:08:25.481
This was one of the major drivers of
the study of graph theory

00:08:25.481 --> 00:08:27.373
or network theory.

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:30.200
The ideas behind these
theories are now ubiquitous.

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:34.190
We use graphs and networks to describe
the way disease spreads and the way

00:08:34.190 --> 00:08:35.900
computer networks are connected.

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:40.490
While the four color theorem might
seem like a mathematical curiosity,

00:08:40.700 --> 00:08:44.150
it really did push the boundaries
of mathematics forward.

